     - Auto checkbox starts unchecked for each raid and only stays checked if the user clicks it. When checked, the banner jumps to an existing target split or opens a new main-window tab when the raid executes, once per raid from a visible split.

8. **TwitchPipe stream player backend**
   - **Feature**: External Tools has a Stream player choice between Streamlink and TwitchPipe. TwitchPipe has its own path, quality, config file, and extra options. Choose quality runs `twitchpipe --streams` and reuses the existing quality popup.
   - **Files Modified**:
     - `src/singletons/Settings.hpp` - `StreamPlayerBackend` and `TwitchPipeQuality` settings, plus magic_enum display names for the TwitchPipe quality values.
     - `src/util/StreamLink.hpp/.cpp` - Backend-aware binary path, not-found dialog, process setup, `--streams` listing, and argument building. `openStreamlink` and `openStreamlinkForChannelOrUrl` stay the public API.
     - `src/widgets/dialogs/QualityPopup.cpp` - `showDialog` sorts the option list before filling the combo box.
     - `src/widgets/settingspages/SettingWidget.hpp/.cpp` - `conditionallyEnabledBy(EnumStringSetting<T>&, T)` and dropdown instantiations for the new enums.
     - `src/widgets/settingspages/ExternalToolsPage.cpp` - Stream player section with a backend dropdown and Streamlink / TwitchPipe groups. The page uses `SettingsPage::managedConnections_` for the enable binding.
     - `tests/CMakeLists.txt` - Registers `tests/src/StreamLink.cpp`.
   - **Files Added**:
     - `src/util/StreamQualitySort.hpp/.cpp` - `sortStreamQualities` ranks Choose-popup labels for both backends.
     - `tests/src/StreamLink.cpp` - Order checks for Choose-popup quality labels.
   - **Behavior Notes**:
     - Quality listing runs `twitchpipe --log-level error --streams <channel>`, optionally with `--config` when the config path is set. Each non-empty stdout line is one quality. Streamlink still parses the `Available streams:` line and strips its ` (best)` / ` (worst)` markers before the popup sees the names.
     - Both Choose popups run that list through `sortStreamQualities` in `QualityPopup::showDialog`. Resolution labels go highest height first, and a higher frame rate breaks a tie (`1080p60` before `1080p`, `720p60` before `720p`). A trailing ` (...)` suffix such as ` (source)` is ignored for rank and kept on the selectable string. `best` is first, unrecognized labels keep their relative order, `worst` follows those, and `audio`, `audio_only`, and `audio-only` are last. Duplicate labels are kept.
     - Non-zero exit posts the last stderr line as a system message on the split that started the launch, and logs a warning. Offline channels typically end with `Error: no streams found on channel X`.
     - A bare login is passed through. A URL with no scheme, including `twitch.tv/<name>`, gets `https://` prepended. That rewrite also happens inside `openStreamlink` when TwitchPipe is the backend at launch time, so the quality popup follows the backend selected when OK is clicked.
     - Preferred quality maps to `best`, no quality argument (config priority list), or the custom priority string. An empty custom list falls back to `best`. Popup quality names are passed through unchanged, including labels like `1080p60 (source)`.
     - `--config` is sent only when the config file setting is non-empty. Otherwise TwitchPipe uses its own lookup, next to the executable and then the working directory.
     - A normal Streamlink launch still gets the same arguments. The chat system message is `Opening <channel> in Streamlink ...` or `Opening <channel> in TwitchPipe ...`.
     - Upstream #7355 launches Streamlink attached and shows `StreamLinkErrorPopup` with its output on a non-zero exit. The fork keeps detached launches for both backends, so only the Streamlink quality listing uses that popup (stdout/stderr merged, shown when the last line isn't `Available streams:`). A player that fails after it starts is not reported.
     - Attached quality queries are deleted when they exit. FailedToStart shows one not-found dialog and deletes the process. Detached launches use `startDetached`, show that dialog only from the false return, and delete the `QProcess` without waiting for the player to exit.
     - Holding Shift while starting a launch with quality `Choose` skips the popup and uses `best` for both backends. Hotkeys whose binding lacks Shift will not fire with Shift held, so menu and button triggers are the intended path.
     - The menu label "Open in streamlink", the `/streamlink` command, and the `openInStreamlink` hotkey id are unchanged.

9. **Replay chat via mpv**
   - TwitchPipe sends HLS segment program-date ends through its byte buffer, observes mpv IPC playback and cache timestamps, and reports mapped wall-clock positions over a reconnecting websocket client. Its `[replay]` config defaults to disabled.
   - `src/controllers/replay/ReplayServer.hpp/.cpp` listens on localhost port 38776 by default, and `ReplayController.hpp/.cpp` tracks player instances by channel. `Application` exposes the controller; `EmptyApplication` returns null.
   - `ChannelView::scrollToServerTime` uses the filtered snapshot and a cached timestamp index. Routine replay scroll advances by at most five messages per 50 ms step; explicit seeks jump straight to the requested time. `Split` binds to one player per Twitch channel, snaps to normal live scrolling with 15/20 second hysteresis, and shows green, blue, or orange borders for replay, live, or waiting.
   - Ctrl+Alt+R and the split More menu toggle replay. External Tools contains the listener port and live thresholds. Replay mode is per split and is cleared on channel changes.
   - Replay-follow suppresses "More messages below". Pausing chat and scrolling manually restores it; normal mode retains its existing banner behavior. TwitchPipe logs connection changes to mpv's backtick console, including changes before the console first opens.
   - When TwitchPipe uses `player.no_close = true`, its replay reporter remains active after the stream ends and stops only after mpv exits, so cached-video seeks keep moving chat.

10. **Open most recent VOD in mpv**
   - The Twitch split header menu has "Open most recent VOD", below "Open in streamlink". It runs `mpv <vod url>` detached, using `mpv` from PATH with no other arguments.
   - `Helix::getLatestArchiveVideo` (`src/providers/twitch/api/Helix.hpp/.cpp`, mock in `mocks/include/mocks/Helix.hpp`) fetches `videos?type=archive&first=1` for the channel's room ID. `Split::openLatestVodInMpv` reports missing VODs, Helix errors, and mpv launch failures as system messages.


11. **Hype trains, polls and predictions**
   - **Feature**: Stacked bars under the raid banner for the channel's hype train (any kind: normal, Treasure, Golden Kappa, shared), poll and prediction. Each one shows and hides on its own, so several can run at once. Polls can be voted on (free vote only) and predictions made from the bars. Chat gets a system message with the final numbers when a hype train, poll or prediction ends.
   - **Files Added**:
     - `src/providers/twitch/ChannelEvents.hpp/.cpp` - Side-effect-free state structs, PubSub/GQL parsers, GQL request builders, end-of-event chat text, and `ChannelEventsTracker`, which turns messages into current state plus once-only "ended" results.
     - `src/widgets/splits/ChannelEventsBanner.hpp/.cpp` - The stacked bars (`channeleventsbanner::EventBar`, `ShareRow`, `ProgressStrip`, `ElidedLabel`), vote confirmation, and the sign-in prompt.
     - `src/widgets/splits/PredictionDialog.hpp/.cpp` - Amount entry with quick amounts, payout estimate, balance before/after, and a lock countdown. The dialog is the confirmation.
     - `tests/src/ChannelEvents.cpp` - Fixtures trimmed from real `hype-train-events-v2` / `predictions-channel-v1` / `polls` messages and real vote/prediction responses.
     - `scripts/twitch-events-capture.user.js` - Tampermonkey script that records twitch.tv GQL and Hermes/PubSub traffic for polls, predictions and hype trains (tokens redacted) to check shapes against.
   - **Files Modified**:
     - `src/providers/twitch/PubSubManager.hpp/.cpp` - `listenToChannelEvents()` (topics `hype-train-events-v2`, `polls`, `predictions-channel-v1`; no auth needed) and `channelEvents.{hypeTrain,poll,prediction}` signals.
     - `src/providers/twitch/PubSubClient.cpp` - Dispatches those topics as (channelId, type, data).
     - `src/providers/twitch/TwitchIrcServer.cpp` - Routes the signals onto `TwitchChannel::handle*Message` on the GUI thread.
     - `src/providers/twitch/TwitchChannel.hpp/.cpp` - Owns the tracker, fetches an unauthenticated GQL snapshot on join and when PubSub shows we're out of date (5s minimum gap), posts the end messages, and has a `channelEventsChanged` signal.
     - `src/controllers/channelpoints/ChannelPointsController.hpp/.cpp` - `isSignedIn()`, `voteInPoll()` (`ChannelPollContext_VoteInPoll`) and `makePrediction()`, both persisted hashes with inline fallback, using the channel points sign-in.
     - `src/widgets/splits/Split.hpp/.cpp` - Inserts `eventsBanner_` after `raidBanner_`. The pinned, raid and events banners sit in one `banners_` container so `setBannersHidden()` can hide them all.
     - `src/widgets/splits/SplitHeader.cpp` - "More > Show top banners" (checkable, per split).
     - Pinned messages for non-mods: `TwitchChannel::refreshPinnedMessage()` uses Helix `chat/pins` only when the user has mod rights (it returns 403 "does not have the required permission" otherwise) and falls back to the website's unauthenticated GQL `pinnedChatMessages` via `fetchPinnedMessageFromGql()` (also when Helix fails or the user is anonymous). `channelevents::buildPinnedMessageRequest/parsePinnedMessage` turn the GQL node into a `HelixPinnedChatMessage`; paid (non-`MOD`) and expired pins are skipped.
     - `src/common/WindowDescriptors.hpp/.cpp`, `src/widgets/splits/SplitContainer.cpp` - Saves/loads the per-split `bannersHidden` flag in the window layout (missing = shown).
     - `tests/src/SplitDescriptor.cpp` - Round-trip of `bannersHidden`.
     - `src/CMakeLists.txt`, `tests/CMakeLists.txt` - New sources.
   - **Behavior Notes**:
     - Seeing events needs no login. Voting and predicting need the channel points sign-in; without it the bar offers to open the channel points popup.
     - Every vote asks first. A prediction only happens from the dialog's "Predict N points" button. Paid extra poll votes (`tokens.channelPoints`) are intentionally not implemented.
     - `hype-train-end` carries only `{id, ended_at, ending_reason}`, so the end message uses the last progress seen. `ending_reason: EXPIRED` means level 1 was never completed. A `hype-train-progression` for an unknown train triggers a snapshot.
     - Ended polls and predictions keep their results on the bar for 30s (`RESULT_DISPLAY_SECONDS`). Prediction status only moves forward (active → locked → pending → ended), so out-of-order updates (an `ACTIVE` one for a last-second prediction after `LOCKED`, `CANCEL_PENDING` after `CANCELED`) are ignored, and repeats are never announced twice. If `LOCKED` hasn't arrived 2s after the window ends, `expire()` locks it locally; the dialog stops accepting predictions as soon as the timer hits 0.
     - The client remembers its own votes and predictions for the session only (`recordPollVote` / `recordPrediction`); votes made elsewhere aren't shown (deliberately; `predictions-user-v1` isn't used). The prediction bar header shows your stake and the outcome you picked, in its color.
     - Bars can be collapsed (click the header) or hidden per event (×). Polls and predictions start expanded until you take part, then collapse to a one-line summary.


12. **Highlight-only ignore phrases**
   - **Feature**: A blocking ignore phrase can be marked "Highlight-only". It then never hides the message; when a highlighted message matches, it can independently keep that message out of `/mentions` ("Suppress Mentions") and drop its highlight color ("Suppress Color"). Sounds and taskbar alerts still fire.
   - **Files Modified**:
     - `src/controllers/ignores/IgnorePhrase.hpp/.cpp` - `highlightOnly`, `suppressMentions`, `suppressHighlight` fields, serialized under the same names (missing = false).
     - `src/controllers/ignores/IgnoreController.hpp/.cpp` - `isIgnoredMessage` skips highlight-only phrases; `checkIgnoredHighlights()` ORs the suppress options of every matching highlight-only phrase.
     - `src/controllers/ignores/IgnoreModel.cpp`, `src/widgets/settingspages/IgnoresPage.cpp` - Three extra checkbox columns.
     - `src/messages/MessageFlag.hpp` - `DoNotShowInMentions` (1 << 47) and `DisableHighlightColor` (1 << 48).
     - `src/messages/MessageBuilder.cpp` - `makeIrcMessage` checks the phrases before `parseHighlights()` and sets the flags afterwards, only when the message ended up `Highlighted`.
     - `src/messages/Message.cpp`, `src/messages/layouts/MessageLayout.cpp` - `DisableHighlightColor` hides the scrollbar marker and background color.
     - `src/providers/twitch/IrcMessageHandler.cpp`, `src/providers/twitch/TwitchChannel.cpp` - `DoNotShowInMentions` is checked wherever IRC messages and recent messages are copied into `/mentions`.
     - `tests/src/IgnoreController.cpp`, `tests/src/IrcMessageHandler.cpp` - Phrase round-trip and mentions/color suppression.
   - **Behavior Notes**:
     - Only the IRC message path (`makeIrcMessage`) applies these flags. Upstream's subgift and EventSub automod paths add to `/mentions` directly and are not affected.
     - Since upstream's highlight rework (#6695), every highlight type sets `MessageFlag::Highlighted` (`HighlightedWhisper` is gone), including first messages, redemptions, subscriptions and announcements. Suppression therefore applies to those too, not only to user/message/badge highlights.

13. **Not-connected tab indicator**
   - **Feature**: A tab with a Twitch split whose channel isn't joined gets a 2px orange line (selected-tab thickness, on whichever edge the tab bar uses) and a half-transparent title. Lets you watch tabs connect at startup and see which ones are back after a network drop. Toggle: Settings > General > "Mark tabs with channels that aren't connected" (`/appearance/showTabNotJoined`, default on).
   - **Files Modified**:
     - `src/providers/twitch/TwitchChannel.hpp/.cpp` - `isJoined()` and `joinedStatusChanged`. Set by our own JOIN (the existing `joined` signal), cleared by `markDisconnected()` when the read connection drops. Channels starting with `/` (plugin channels, never joined) always count as joined.
     - `src/widgets/helper/ChannelView.hpp/.cpp` - Forwards it as `joinedStatusChanged`, like `liveStatusChanged`.
     - `src/widgets/splits/SplitContainer.hpp/.cpp` - `refreshTabJoinedStatus()` on that signal and in `refreshTab()` (split added/removed, channel changed).
     - `src/widgets/helper/NotebookTab.hpp/.cpp` - `setNotJoined()` and the paint.
     - `src/singletons/Settings.hpp`, `src/widgets/settingspages/GeneralPage.cpp` - The setting.
   - **Behavior Notes**:
     - Only our own JOIN counts. ROOMSTATE/`setRoomId` is not used because `MessageBuilder` also sets the room ID from recent-messages history.
     - Non-Twitch splits (mentions, whispers, live, automod, empty) never mark the tab.
     - A channel Twitch refuses to join (suspended, nonexistent) stays marked, since no JOIN ever arrives.
     - A silently dead connection only shows once Communi notices (ping timeout) and emits `disconnected`.
